The video posted on Friday shows a screenshot of this alleged proposal, dated May 13, 2026, and states that &ldquo;Our provisional plan is to release the Franky mix in the next six weeks. Franky&rsquo;s version will have very minimal orchestral elements and the vocal will be a straight replay of the original. For John&rsquo;s mix, we would like to release this later in the summer (although totally open to discussing timelines with you guys of course).&rdquo;

Another screenshot, dated May 23, shows a message from Summit&rsquo;s team noting that they hadn&rsquo;t yet had a chance to discuss the project with him. Tong says his own team pursued a response through mid-June but did not receive a follow-up, adding: &ldquo;We never received a session from John.&rdquo;

A statement provided to Billboard by Tong&rsquo;s team expands on these explanations: &ldquo;This week John publicly accused Pete of having copied or stolen his remix. Those allegations are simply not supported by the facts,&rdquo; it reads.

The statement continues that the Rizardo edit, which was played in Rizardo&rsquo;s June 20 Essential Mix on BBC Radio, is a &ldquo;newly recorded production created with Pete Tong and his label, with all musical elements re-recorded, including the vocal.&rdquo; The statement also says that Tong and his team tried contacting Summit and his team regarding the situation but have not yet received a response.&nbsp;

In the video Tong says &ldquo;I&rsquo;m disappointed. I don&rsquo;t like being accused of things that aren&rsquo;t true. If you&rsquo;ve got a problem, pick up the phone. That&rsquo;s certainly what I would preach. I&rsquo;ve tried to call John, I&rsquo;ve tried to text him; he&rsquo;s chosen to not respond &mdash; And that&rsquo;s why I&rsquo;m here today, to make sure everyone has the context of how we got to this point.&rdquo; Read the complete statement below.

Meanwhile, &ldquo;Missing&rdquo; has been a staple in Summit&rsquo;s sets over the last several months, and he seems to be preparing his own orchestral version for his sold-out Tofte Manor show this weekend, July 11-12. Billboard has reached out to Summit&rsquo;s team for comment.

Tong, a key dance world figure since the 1990s, played the inaugural edition of Summit&rsquo;s Experts Only festival on Randall&rsquo;s Island in New York last September.&nbsp;

Complete statement from the team of Pete Tong:

&ldquo;Over the last week there has been a lot of discussion online regarding Pete Tong, &lsquo;Missing&rsquo; by Everything But The Girl, and comments made by John Summit. Because social media rarely allows enough space for context, we want to set out the facts as clearly as possible.

&ldquo;Pete has long wanted to include a version of &lsquo;Missing in Ibiza Classics,&rsquo; which returns for a UK arena tour this December. As part of that process, Pete became aware of two separate club versions of the track that were being played by DJs: one associated with Franky Rizardo and another associated with John Summit. Like many contemporary DJ versions of classic records, these were based around existing elements of the original recording and reflected the continuing popularity of an iconic song.

&ldquo;Before pursuing any commercial release, Pete contacted the representatives of Everything But The Girl to understand Ben Watt and Tracey Thorn&rsquo;s position regarding new interpretations and remixes of the song. Pete also spoke directly with both Franky Rizardo and John Summit about the possibility of creating newly recorded versions that would comply with the guidance provided.

&ldquo;A version was subsequently developed with Franky Rizardo. Importantly, this was not a release of an existing DJ remix. It was a newly recorded production created with Pete Tong and his label, with all musical elements re-recorded, including the vocal. The arrangement reflected the style of the version Franky had created and been playing in his sets, but did not contain orchestral elements and did not use John Summit&rsquo;s work.

&ldquo;At the same time, Pete and John Summit discussed a separate concept involving an orchestral interpretation of &lsquo;Missing&rsquo; for Ibiza Classics. Those conversations were positive but ultimately did not progress beyond an initial exchange of ideas. By mid-May we were informed that John&rsquo;s team had been unable to engage him further on the project, and the discussions effectively came to a halt.

&ldquo;This week John publicly accused Pete of having copied or stolen his remix. Those allegations are simply not supported by the facts. The Franky Rizardo recording was developed independently, through a process that was openly communicated to the relevant parties. The communications between Pete&rsquo;s label and John Summit&rsquo;s management team document that process and show that discussions regarding potential versions of the song were taking place transparently and in good faith.

